<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1500–1600 (the city of Venice in the 16th century)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Doge to the Ghetto</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/venice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venice.jpg" alt="Venice"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a><span class="soc-credit">kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede">The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ranked its people by birth. Its society was divided into three bodies: the patricians, the citizens and the foreigners. Since the lockout (Serrata) of the Great Council in 1297, only the men of the noble families enrolled in the Golden Book sat in the Great Council, the supreme body of the state, and filled its councils and offices; the doge, elected for life from among them, reigned within strict limits. The citizens were a middle order created after the Serrata, and those born to old Venetian families staffed the state’s chancellery and the professions. Below them lived the broad mass of the common people (popolo): fishermen, salt workers, glassmakers, shipwrights and other workers, most of a city of 124,000 to 175,000 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, denied citizenship, were confined from 1516 to the Ghetto.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1500–1600 (the city of Venice in the 16th century)</span><span><b>People</b> 124,000–175,000</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 7</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Doge</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:43%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Patriziato</span> <span class="soc-en">(Patricians)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 3–6% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:62%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Clergy</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:6%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Cittadini</span> <span class="soc-en">(Citizens)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 5–8% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:81%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:82%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Popolo</span> <span class="soc-en">(Common people)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 82% (the rest, est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:3%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Foreign communities</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–4% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-outcast soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Jews of the Ghetto</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–3% (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/francesco-foscari.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Doge</span></h3><p class="soc-term">dux</p><p class="soc-kid">The doge was the head of Venice, chosen for life, but the councils kept a close watch on everything he did.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The doge was head of state for life. From 1268 forty-one electors chose him at the end of a long chain of lots and votes in the Great Council, designed to limit the influence of the great families. He promulgated the laws and commanded the army in war. Yet he swore an oath, the promissione ducale, recognising the limits of his power, and he was closely watched: he could not open dispatches from foreign powers without other officials present, own property abroad or name his successor, and after his death inquisitors judged his acts and could fine his estate. On Ascension Day he cast a ring into the sea from the state barge, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucentaur">Bucentaur</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The doge was elected from among the patricians, usually a man of the inner circle of powerful families. Most ruled until death, but a few were forced out, and in 1355 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> condemned Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marino-faliero">Marino Faliero</a> to death for trying to make himself lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, elected for life.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucentaur">Bucentaur</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marriage-of-the-sea-ceremony">Marriage of the Sea ceremony</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 3–6% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Patriziato</span> <span class="soc-en">(Patricians)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">patrizio, nobil homo, case vecchie, case nuove</p><p class="soc-kid">Noble men ran the government of Venice together, and their names were written in a register called the Golden Book.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The patricians alone sat in the Great Council. After the Serrata of 1297 and the laws of 1319–1323 membership was hereditary, and every nobleman entered the Council at 25. The families were registered in the Golden Book (Libro d’Oro), whose records of noble births and marriages, begun in 1506 and 1526, were kept by the Avogadori de Comùn. In law all patricians were equal: every vote, the doge’s included, counted the same. In black togas they filled every council and magistracy, governed the subject cities as podestà and commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, mostly for a year and for little pay. They drew their wealth from trade with the East rather than from land, and some 20 to 30 families dominated the state.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth alone made a patrician: a son of a noble father registered in the Golden Book, but not a son born to a mother of lower status. From 1498 a noble who followed a career in the Church lost his seat, and new families were admitted only rarely, as in 1381 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: 2,746 noblemen were entitled to sit in the Great Council in 1527, and up to 2,095 attended a session in the 16th century (all men of 25 and over); with their wives and children perhaps 6,000–9,000 people in a city of 124,000–175,000.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libro-doro">Libro d'Oro</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serrata-del-maggior-consiglio">Serrata del Maggior Consiglio</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-nobility">Venetian nobility</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avogadoria-de-comun">Avogadoria de Comùn</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Portrait of a Venetian patrician, by Tintoretto · Domenico Tintoretto / Jacopo Tintoretto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tintoretto_-_Portrait_of_a_Venetian_Patrician%2C_68.97.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-3"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-3.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Clergy</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Priests, monks and nuns served Venice’s many churches, but the government kept a close eye on what they said.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was the state religion, but the Republic kept its clergy in check. In 1451 Pope Nicholas V erected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patriarchate-of-venice">Patriarchate of Venice</a>, whose cathedral was San Pietro di Castello, not St Mark’s. The government regulated the building of churches, limited bequests to the Church and watched the priests who preached against it, sometimes expelling them from the state. The Inquisition was active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1542 and tried 3,620 defendants before 1794, but lay magistrates sat at its trials, sometimes softened its punishments, and it had no power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> or other non-Christians. Every Easter Monday the abbess of San Zaccaria presented the doge with a new linen cap made by her nuns.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> From 1498 a noble who chose a career in the Church was barred from the Great Council, so a patrician’s son had to choose between the councils and the clergy.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the priests, monks and nuns of the city’s parishes and convents; the articles give no count.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patriarchate-of-venice">Patriarchate of Venice</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The doge and the patriarch of Venice welcome King Henry III of France, painted by Andrea Vicentino · Andrea Vicentino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Vicentino_-_The_Doge_and_the_Patriarch_Welcoming_Henri_III%2C_King_of_France_(detail)_-_WGA25056.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-4"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-4.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 5–8% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Cittadini</span> <span class="soc-en">(Citizens)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">cittadini originari, cittadini de intra, cittadini de extra</p><p class="soc-kid">Citizens were the middle rank of Venice: they worked as the state’s secretaries, lawyers and notaries.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">After the Serrata closed the Great Council, the citizens (cittadini) were instituted as a middle order between the nobility and the common people, a place for ambitious families of wealth and distinction. Original citizens (cittadini originari), natives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> or of Venetian families to the third degree, could hold public office: they served as chancellors, secretaries, lawyers and notaries. Families of ancient Venetian origin and of “civilization” and “honour” were listed in a Silver Book and staffed the state bureaucracy, above all the chancellery in the Doge’s Palace. Citizens joined together in scholae, religious and professional confraternities that gave their members mutual aid and set the rules of the trades. Newcomers could be granted citizenship on merit.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A family were citizens by birth, as natives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> or of Venetian families to the third degree, or by a grant on merit: de intra to work within the city, de extra to sail and trade abroad as Venetians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were excluded.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the citizens by birth with their families, and those granted citizenship; the articles give no count, and this middle order is taken here as about one to two times as many people as the nobility.</p></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Venetian citizens or merchants, from Cesare Vecellio's book of costumes · Cesare Vecellio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cittadini_o_mercanti_Venetiani_in_Soria_-_Vecellio_Cesare_-_1860.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-5"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/murano" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/murano.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 82% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Popolo</span> <span class="soc-en">(Common people)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most Venetians were fishermen, salt workers, shipbuilders, glassmakers and other workers.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The common people (popolo) were the great majority of the city. In the lagoon they fished and salted the catch and worked the salt pans, which the great families owned and rented to salt workers’ families, who maintained them and organised themselves in consortia. Glass was made only on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murano">Murano</a>, to keep fires out of the city, and shipwrights built and armed the fleet in the state Arsenal; others made naval rope from imported hemp, tanned hides and spun wool on the Giudecca. Tailors and other trades were organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, and by the end of the 16th century some 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> businesses worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The people’s old assembly, the Concio, had been abolished in 1423.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The Great Council was closed to commoners except in rare emergencies: in 1381 thirty commoners who had given most to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> were made nobles. A family of wealth and distinction could hope to rise into the citizens.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone else: fishermen, salt workers, craftsmen, shipbuilders, labourers and their families, the great majority of the city. The other shares are estimates, so this one is too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murano">Murano</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fondaco-dei-tedeschi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 2–4% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Foreign communities</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Greeks, Germans, Armenians and Ottoman merchants lived or traded in Venice, and the Greeks had a church of their own.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Venetian society counted foreigners as a body of their own, beside the patricians and the citizens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> had the church of San Biagio from 1456, the only church where they could celebrate their rites, and in 1514, when it grew overcrowded, were allowed to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-giorgio-dei-greci">San Giorgio dei Greci</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> had lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> since the mid-13th century. Ottoman merchants stayed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-turchi">Fondaco dei Turchi</a>, which held a small mosque, but did not settle. Foreigners led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> trade: the German Johann of Speyer obtained the first printing privilege in 1469, and by the end of the 15th century the German and French communities, the French led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-jenson">Nicolas Jenson</a>, owned most of the city’s presses.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A newcomer could be granted Venetian citizenship on merit, to work within the city or to sail and trade as a Venetian; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were excluded.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the Greek, German, Armenian and other communities of the city; the articles give no count. In 1770 Orthodox Greeks were 13.3 per cent of the whole Republic, most of them in its lands overseas.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-jenson">Nicolas Jenson</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-and-wendelin-of-speyer">Johann and Wendelin of Speyer</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi">Fondaco dei Tedeschi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-giorgio-dei-greci">San Giorgio dei Greci</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-turchi">Fondaco dei Turchi</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-outcast" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/venice-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Outcast groups</span> · <span>c. 1–3% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Jews of the Ghetto</span></h3><p class="soc-term">ghetto</p><p class="soc-kid">Venice’s Jews had to live in one district, the Ghetto, and wear a red cloth, but they had their own synagogues there.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had no Venetian citizenship and for long no right to live in the city. Moneylenders, whose loans the city’s business needed, were admitted with permits renewed every five or ten years, and were first allowed to live in the lagoon in 1385; Jews had to wear a distinctive sign, a yellow circle that in the 16th century became a red cloth. After the defeat at Agnadello in 1509 brought refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> and Conegliano and tensions with the Christians grew, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-ghetto">Venetian Ghetto</a> was established on 29 March 1516 and enlarged in 1541 for the Levantine Jews. Under the magistrates in Cattaver they ran the Ghetto’s pawnshops and traded in second-hand goods. Each community of origin had its own synagogue.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth decided it: the law excluded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Venetian citizenship, and the Ghetto was their only lawful home in the city.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the communities of the Ghetto, founded in 1516 and enlarged for the Levantine Jews in 1541; the articles give no count for the 16th century, and the census of 1770 found 5,026 Jews, about 0.2 per cent of the whole Republic.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetian-ghetto">Venetian Ghetto</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: One of the synagogues of the Ghetto of Venice · DaringDonna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_of_the_synagogues_of_the_Jewish_Ghetto_of_Venice.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="venice">Be born in the city of Venice</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>’s ladder was closed at the top. The Serrata of 1297 first widened the Great Council, which more than doubled to over 1,100 members by 1300, and then shut it: by 1323 membership was hereditary, and the new ruling class of almost 200 families monopolised the higher offices. Not everyone accepted it: in 1300 a commoner, Marin Bocconio, who thought he should have been admitted, was hanged for plotting to kill Doge Pietro Gradenigo. The citizens were created as the way up for families of wealth and distinction, and the nobility opened again only in emergencies, as in 1381, when thirty commoners who had given most to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> were made nobles; mainland families who gave the Republic military support received the title too, mostly as an honour. In the 15th and 16th centuries the aristocracy was numerous and poor nobles a minority, and trade with the East and the new workshops of the lagoon could raise a noble family quickly. Later the gates opened for money: in the 17th and 18th centuries the state sold the title for 100,000 ducats, and 134 families were admitted.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>A woman took her rank from her family and her husband. Noblewomen bore the title Nobilis Domina (N.D.) but had no seat in the Great Council, which only noble men attended. The law forbade nobles to marry outside the nobility, and a son born to a mother of lower status could not sit in the Council; the Golden Book recorded noble marriages as well as births. Marriage ties with the old houses had helped some commoner families to be chosen for the nobility in 1381. Noblewomen dressed in long embroidered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> with trailing cloaks and furs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> laws from 1488 and, from 1514, a magistracy watching spending on parties and clothes tried to curb the expense. The nuns of San Zaccaria made the doge’s linen cap each year.</p></section>
